From e05ed3cee7819feefb8e30256441ab10c62612bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:05:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Aurora: HD look fixes for chrissotraidis/bluewake#491: AO
 halo, bloom on clouds, flickering shadows, the far blur and lightning

Blur and upscale the HD look's ambient occlusion along each surface.

The occlusion is worked out at a quarter of the size, then blurred and
stretched back to the scene's size. Both steps mixed in neighbours
whatever their distance, so Link's own shade (under his hat, around his
shield) spread about 30 pixels onto the sea behind him: a dark outline
around him (bluewake #491).

The blur and the upscale now weigh each neighbour by how near its
distance is to the pixel's, so a shade stays on its own surface. Where
there is no depth (the sky, the horizon) the scene is left as it is.
The blur takes whole texels, one apart, to read each one's distance.
Sun shadows keep the shared blur and apply.

Fade the HD look's ambient occlusion out with distance.

Toward the horizon the occlusion's ring is a few pixels across, and the
sea's swells darkened it in a band from about 1,500 to 8,000 near-plane
units, right up to Link's outline on Outset's pier (bluewake #491).
It is now full up to 1,000 units and gone by 2,500, and pixels beyond
skip the work. DOL_AURORA_AO_FADE_START and DOL_AURORA_AO_FADE_END move
the two distances.

Screen the HD look's bloom onto the scene instead of adding it.

Added, the glow pushed Wind Waker's near-white clouds past white: at
Outset's pier a fifth of the clouds were flat white, with no shading
left (none with the HD look off). Screened (glow * (1 - scene) +
scene), it brightens what is darker as before and tends to white
without clipping.

The far blur eases off within its reach of the sky when the sky is left sharp.

With the sky left sharp (DOL_AURORA_FAR_BLUR_BACKGROUND=0, BlueWake's HD
look) the blurred picture next to it is part sky: the sea along the horizon
took a bright band, and a distant lightning strike spread into a wide column.
Within the blur's reach of where there is no depth it now blurs as much as the
sky does. Wind Waker HD keeps the horizon line sharp.

A lightning strike's invisible model writes no depth while the HD look's effects are on.

A strike (dThunder_c) draws with its bolt an invisible model that writes depth
only, about 100,000 units out: a strip from the sea to the clouds. The HD
look's effects read the depth, so the far blur and the cloud shadows took that
strip of sky for something solid and drew it as a grey column. A depth-only
draw that far out writes none while they are on; twelve saved states
(sailing, islands, sunrise, sunset) render identically with and without it.

The HD look's effects go in before a copy of the frame laid over it.

Wind Waker blends the previous frame's copy back over the scene while the
camera shakes (motionBlure, as when Link's sword hits a tree). That copy
already holds the HD look's effects, and post_fx ran after it, at the
scene copy or after the last perspective draw: the shadows (and ambient
occlusion and cloud shadows) were multiplied in a second time. Shadowed
ground went from 0.65 of its unshadowed brightness down to 0.41 and back
with every game frame of the blur (bluewake #491).

A flat draw that samples a copy of the EFB is now marked as such, and the
effects go in before the first one that follows the scene's perspective
draws.

The HD look's ambient occlusion only from what is within reach in the scene.

Each pair of samples around a pixel occluded it when their average was
nearer the camera, with the radius measured on screen. Across a floor
seen at a low angle, a short step on screen is a long way along the
floor: Link's legs darkened a wide patch of floor in front of him in a
test room, and long grass at Outset left a dark halo (bluewake #491).

The pixel and each sample now get a place in the scene from the depth,
and the surface's normal from the depth around the pixel (the side
nearer its distance on each axis, so an edge does not bend it). A sample
shades the pixel only when it is above that surface and within the
radius of it in the scene, less with height and distance. A flat surface
is not shaded at any angle; creases and what stands on a surface are.
Twelve samples instead of six pairs, each read at a whole texel of the
depth and placed at that texel's centre, so a sample never mixes two
distances across an edge.

DOL_AURORA_AO_DEBUG=2 tints what the ambient occlusion darkens.

DOL_AURORA_AO_DEBUG=1 shows the occlusion alone, shade on white, which
hides where it lands in the scene. With 2, what it darkens is tinted red
over the scene instead (four times as strong, so a faint shade shows),
as the sun shadows' cascade view tints its cascades.

The sun shadow map's bias near the camera as far off.

Near the camera the lookup moved out half a texel along the surface's
normal (two far off) and took one texel of the surface's slope into the
bias (ten far off). On a surface the sun only grazes (rock walls at
Outset) the map's own texels showed through as vertical stripes, light
and dark, that shifted as the camera moved. The far values near the
camera too: the stripes are gone on both walls, and Link's shadow still
meets his feet (in the first cascade two texels are about a world unit,
and the slope's share is small on ground the sun shines on).
DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_MAP_NORMAL_NEAR=0.5 and _SLOPE_NEAR=1 bring back
the old.

The sun shadow map reads distances from the depth with the camera's far plane.

The scene's depth gave the distance as near / depth, which holds for a far
plane infinitely far. With the camera's own the distance came out too far
by about distance / far: a few units at Link's feet, hundreds at an island
8,000 units off. There the lookup landed behind the island's own sunlit
face, which shaded itself in blocks the size of the cascade's texels
(dark from the waterline up, gone as the boat came closer).
aurora_set_world_view_far() passes the far distance (0 keeps the old
reading), and the shadow map's view_depth() adds near / (far - near) to
the depth before dividing, the exact distance.
